# C# async/await rules (ASP.NET Core)

## 🎯 Primary directive

Prioritize non-blocking, high-throughput async I/O. Eliminate sync-over-async, maximize parallelism, and control concurrency.

## 🚫 Critical anti-patterns (never generate)

### 1) Blocking async code (thread pool starvation)

```csharp
// NEVER block on async - causes thread pool exhaustion
.Result
.Wait()
.GetAwaiter().GetResult()
Task.Run(() => await SomeAsyncMethod()) // unnecessary for I/O operations
```

### 2) Async void (silent failures)

```csharp
// ❌ WRONG - exceptions are swallowed
public async void ProcessMessage(Message msg)

// ✅ CORRECT - use Task instead
public async Task ProcessMessageAsync(Message msg, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
```

### 3) Sequential async when parallel is possible

```csharp
// ❌ INEFFICIENT - sequential execution
var user = await GetUserAsync(id, cancellationToken);
var permissions = await GetPermissionsAsync(id, cancellationToken);

// ✅ EFFICIENT - parallel execution
var userTask = GetUserAsync(id, cancellationToken);
var permissionsTask = GetPermissionsAsync(id, cancellationToken);
await Task.WhenAll(userTask, permissionsTask);
var user = await userTask;
var permissions = await permissionsTask;
```

## ✅ Required patterns

### Async all the way

- Return `Task<T>`/`Task`; avoid `async void`
- Add `CancellationToken cancellationToken = default` to public async APIs
- **ASP.NET Core Note:** `ConfigureAwait(false)` is NOT needed - ASP.NET Core has no SynchronizationContext

### Parallel operations for independent queries

```csharp
// Run independent operations in parallel
var userTask = _userService.GetUserAsync(userId, cancellationToken);
var permissionsTask = _authService.GetPermissionsAsync(userId, cancellationToken);
await Task.WhenAll(userTask, permissionsTask);

var user = await userTask;
var permissions = await permissionsTask;
```

### Controlled concurrency for bulk operations

```csharp
// Limit concurrent operations to prevent resource exhaustion
const int maxConcurrency = 10;
using var semaphore = new SemaphoreSlim(maxConcurrency);

var tasks = ids.Select(async id =>
{
    await semaphore.WaitAsync(cancellationToken);
    try
    {
        return await _service.ProcessAsync(id, cancellationToken);
    }
    finally
    {
        semaphore.Release();
    }
});

var results = await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
```

### Proper exception handling

```csharp
public async Task ProcessAsync(Data data, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
{
    try
    {
        await _repository.SaveAsync(data, cancellationToken);
        await _notificationService.NotifyAsync(data.UserId, cancellationToken);
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Operation cancelled for data {DataId}", data.Id);
        throw; // rethrow cancellation
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        _logger.LogError(ex, "Failed to process data {DataId}", data.Id);
        throw; // rethrow after logging
    }
}
```

### ValueTask for hot paths (optional optimization)

```csharp
// Use ValueTask when operation often completes synchronously
public ValueTask<CachedData?> GetCachedAsync(string key)
{
    if (_cache.TryGetValue(key, out var data))
        return new ValueTask<CachedData?>(data);

    return new ValueTask<CachedData?>(LoadFromDatabaseAsync(key));
}
```

## 🔧 Implementation guidance

### Database (EF Core)

```csharp
// Always use async variants with CancellationToken
var user = await _dbContext.Users
    .FirstOrDefaultAsync(u => u.Id == userId, cancellationToken);

await _dbContext.SaveChangesAsync(cancellationToken);

// Parallel independent queries
var userTask = _dbContext.Users.FindAsync(new object[] { userId }, cancellationToken);
var ordersTask = _dbContext.Orders
    .Where(o => o.UserId == userId)
    .ToListAsync(cancellationToken);

await Task.WhenAll(userTask.AsTask(), ordersTask);
```

### HTTP Clients

```csharp
// Use async HttpClient APIs with timeouts
using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(cancellationToken);
cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));

try
{
    var response = await _httpClient.GetFromJsonAsync<User>(
        $"/api/users/{userId}",
        cts.Token);
    return response;
}
catch (OperationCanceledException)
{
    _logger.LogWarning("HTTP request timed out or was cancelled");
    throw;
}
```

### Transactions

```csharp
// Use async transactions with proper cleanup
await using var transaction = await _dbContext.Database
    .BeginTransactionAsync(cancellationToken);

try
{
    await _dbContext.Users
        .Where(u => u.Id == userId)
        .ExecuteUpdateAsync(/* ... */, cancellationToken);

    await _dbContext.UserAccounts
        .Where(ua => ua.UserId == userId)
        .ExecuteUpdateAsync(/* ... */, cancellationToken);

    await transaction.CommitAsync(cancellationToken);
}
catch
{
    await transaction.RollbackAsync(cancellationToken);
    throw;
}
```

### Controllers/Endpoints

```csharp
// Minimal API endpoints
app.MapGet("/users/{id}", async (
    Guid id,
    IUserService userService,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken) =>
{
    var user = await userService.GetUserAsync(id, cancellationToken);
    return user is not null ? Results.Ok(user) : Results.NotFound();
});

// Controller actions
[HttpGet("{id}")]
public async Task<ActionResult<UserDto>> GetUser(
    Guid id,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    var user = await _userService.GetUserAsync(id, cancellationToken);
    return user is not null ? Ok(user) : NotFound();
}
```

### Services

```csharp
public class UserService : IUserService
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _dbContext;
    private readonly ILogger<UserService> _logger;

    public async Task<User?> GetUserAsync(
        Guid userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        try
        {
            return await _dbContext.Users
                .FirstOrDefaultAsync(u => u.Id == userId, cancellationToken);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _logger.LogError(ex, "Failed to get user {UserId}", userId);
            throw;
        }
    }
}
```

## 🏷️ PublyApp-specific patterns

### Handlers (Minimal API)

```csharp
public static async Task<Results<Ok<UserDto>, NotFound, BadRequest<ApiResponse>>> Handle(
    Guid userId,
    IAuthContext auth,
    ILogger<GetUserHandler> logger,
    MainApiDbContext db,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
{
    if (!auth.IsAuthenticated)
        throw new UnauthorizedAccessException();

    var user = await db.User
        .FirstOrDefaultAsync(u => u.Id == userId, cancellationToken);

    if (user is null)
        return TypedResults.NotFound();

    return TypedResults.Ok(new UserDto
    {
        Id = user.Id,
        Email = user.Email,
        Name = user.Name
    });
}
```

### Service with transaction

```csharp
public async Task<UpdateResult> UpdateStaffMemberAsync(
    Guid userId,
    UpdateDocument document,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
{
    await using var transaction = await _dbContext.Database
        .BeginTransactionAsync(cancellationToken);

    try
    {
        var updated = await _dbContext.User
            .Where(u => u.Id == userId)
            .ExecuteUpdateAsync(/* ... */, cancellationToken);

        if (updated == 0)
        {
            await transaction.RollbackAsync(cancellationToken);
            return UpdateResult.NotFound();
        }

        await transaction.CommitAsync(cancellationToken);
        return UpdateResult.Success();
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        await transaction.RollbackAsync(cancellationToken);
        _logger.LogError(ex, "Failed to update user {UserId}", userId);
        return UpdateResult.Failed(ex.Message);
    }
}
```

### Bulk operations with concurrency control

```csharp
public async Task<List<ProcessResult>> ProcessUsersAsync(
    List<Guid> userIds,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
{
    const int maxConcurrency = 10;
    using var semaphore = new SemaphoreSlim(maxConcurrency);

    var tasks = userIds.Select(async userId =>
    {
        await semaphore.WaitAsync(cancellationToken);
        try
        {
            return await ProcessSingleUserAsync(userId, cancellationToken);
        }
        finally
        {
            semaphore.Release();
        }
    });

    return (await Task.WhenAll(tasks)).ToList();
}
```

## ✅ Lint checklist (verify before commit)

- [ ] No `.Result`/`.Wait()`/`.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` blocking calls
- [ ] No `async void` methods (except event handlers)
- [ ] `Task.WhenAll()` used for independent parallel operations
- [ ] `CancellationToken` parameter on all public async methods
- [ ] `SemaphoreSlim` used to limit concurrency in bulk operations
- [ ] Exceptions logged with structured context before rethrowing
- [ ] Transactions use `await using` and explicit rollback on errors
- [ ] EF Core uses async methods: `FindAsync`, `FirstOrDefaultAsync`, `ToListAsync`, `SaveChangesAsync`, `ExecuteUpdateAsync`

## 📊 Expected benefits

- **Response time:** 10-100x faster for I/O-bound operations
- **Throughput:** 50-100x more concurrent requests
- **Thread usage:** ~80% reduction in thread pool pressure
- **Memory allocation:** ~60% less due to efficient async state machines
- **Cloud costs:** 50-70% savings from better resource utilization

## 🔍 Warning signs of problems

- Thread pool starvation with low CPU usage
- Deadlocks during peak traffic (especially if blocking on async)
- Response times grow exponentially under load
- High memory usage from thread accumulation
- Silent failures from unhandled exceptions in async void methods

## ⚠️ ConfigureAwait(false) - NOT NEEDED in ASP.NET Core

**Important:** ASP.NET Core does NOT have a SynchronizationContext. Using `ConfigureAwait(false)` provides **zero benefit** in ASP.NET Core applications and only adds noise.

```csharp
// ❌ UNNECESSARY in ASP.NET Core apps
var user = await _dbContext.Users
    .FirstOrDefaultAsync(u => u.Id == id, cancellationToken)
    .ConfigureAwait(false); // Does nothing!

// ✅ CORRECT for ASP.NET Core
var user = await _dbContext.Users
    .FirstOrDefaultAsync(u => u.Id == id, cancellationToken);
```

**Only use ConfigureAwait(false) when:**
- Building a reusable class library that might be consumed by UI applications (WPF, WinForms, Xamarin)
- You need to be absolutely certain about not capturing context

**For PublyApp:** Skip `ConfigureAwait(false)` entirely. It's cargo cult programming in ASP.NET Core.

---

Remember: async isn't just syntax—it's the foundation of scalable, high-throughput web applications. Write async code that never blocks and runs independent operations in parallel.
